WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.215 --> 00:00:06.564
At the castle Claudius met privately with Laertes to tell him it was Hamlet who had killed Polonius.

2
00:00:06.565 --> 00:00:11.317
"He was trying to kill me, you must understand," added Claudius.

3
00:00:11.318 --> 00:00:18.484
"So I've sent him to England. I couldn't punish him here; his mother—and the people—love him too much."

4
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:23.356
"I still demand revenge for my father's death," Laertes insisted.

5
00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:28.285
"Don't worry," said the king. "I have more to tell you concerning Hamlet's fate—"

6
00:00:28.286 --> 00:00:32.412
Just then the king was interrupted by the arrival of a messenger.

7
00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:38.262
"A letter from Prince Hamlet," the messenger announced. He handed the king a letter and left.

8
00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:43.904
Claudius was stunned. He was sure that Hamlet had been executed by now in England!

9
00:00:43.905 --> 00:00:48.409
He read the letter. "He says he wants to see me tomorrow," he said.

10
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.349
"Let him come," replied Laertes grimly.

11
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:57.896
"How far are you prepared to go to avenge your father's death?" asked Claudius.

12
00:00:57.897 --> 00:01:02.078
"I would cut his throat in church," said Laertes.

13
00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:08.040
"I have an idea," said Claudius. "We'll set up a fencing match between the two of you.

14
00:01:08.041 --> 00:01:12.444
Hamlet will get a sword with a blunt tip, as is customary.

15
00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:16.484
Your sword will be sharp, but no one will know.

16
00:01:16.485 --> 00:01:23.007
You're an excellent swordsman—you'll mortally wound him, and even his mother will think it was an accident.

17
00:01:23.008 --> 00:01:26.968
So you won't be blamed, but you'll have your revenge."

18
00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:36.420
"I'll do it," Laertes said, his eyes glittering. "And to be sure I kill him, I'll apply a powerful poison to the tip of my sword.

19
00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:40.793
If my sword just scratches him, Hamlet will die."

20
00:01:40.794 --> 00:01:50.827
Claudius looked thoughtful. "But in case you're not able to wound him, I'll have some poisoned wine ready. When Hamlet calls for a drink, we'll—"

21
00:01:50.828 --> 00:01:58.081
At the sound of footsteps outside the door, Claudius quickly fell silent. Gertrude entered, looking anguished.

22
00:01:58.082 --> 00:02:01.620
"Another terrible tragedy," she said.

23
00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:09.699
"Your sister is dead, Laertes! She was sitting on a low willow branch by the stream and weaving wildflowers into garlands.

24
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.898
But the branch broke. She fell into the water and drowned."

25
00:02:13.899 --> 00:02:21.041
"Poor Ophelia," wailed Laertes. "My tears are smothering the fire of my rage.

26
00:02:21.042 --> 00:02:27.207
But when my tears are done, the fire will blaze!" And he ran from the room.

27
00:02:27.208 --> 00:02:35.127
Meanwhile Horatio had met up with Hamlet, and near Elsinore Castle, they saw a funeral procession entering the graveyard.

28
00:02:35.128 --> 00:02:39.479
"It's the king and queen and the court," Hamlet observed with surprise.

29
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:46.589
Then he saw the body being lowered into the grave. "It's Ophelia!" he said, devastated.

30
00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:53.962
Laertes stepped out of the procession and cried, "Curses on the man who caused my beloved sister such pain!

31
00:02:53.963 --> 00:02:58.684
I must hold her one more time!" And he leaped into the grave.

32
00:02:58.685 --> 00:03:05.776
When Laertes saw Hamlet approach the grave, he climbed out and thundered, "The devil take your soul!"

33
00:03:05.777 --> 00:03:15.528
"I loved Ophelia," said Hamlet. "The love of forty thousand brothers could not equal mine. If I could, I'd be buried with her!"

34
00:03:15.529 --> 00:03:21.260
"He does not want to die. This is his madness speaking," Gertrude lamented.

35
00:03:21.261 --> 00:03:24.838
"Horatio, take Hamlet away," Claudius ordered.

36
00:03:24.839 --> 00:03:30.998
To Laertes, he whispered, "Be patient. We'll put our plan into action tonight!"

37
00:03:30.999 --> 00:03:36.094
Back at Elsinore Castle, Hamlet told Horatio more about his journey.

38
00:03:36.095 --> 00:03:43.013
"I was curious about the letter Claudius had given to Rosencrantz and Guildenstern for the king of England," he said.

39
00:03:43.014 --> 00:03:49.870
"So when they were sleeping, I carefully opened it. It was an order to have me beheaded!"

40
00:03:49.871 --> 00:03:53.164
"No!" cried Horatio, shocked.

41
00:03:53.165 --> 00:04:00.576
"So I wrote a new letter and sealed it with my father's ring. My letter ordered the deaths of those two fools."

42
00:04:00.577 --> 00:04:04.522
Horatio looked even more shocked. "They are dead?"

43
00:04:04.523 --> 00:04:10.235
"They were spies, Horatio!" Hamlet retorted. "They got what they deserved."

44
00:04:10.236 --> 00:04:18.252
A young nobleman entered the room and said, "My lord, the king has arranged a fencing match between you and Laertes.

45
00:04:18.253 --> 00:04:24.948
His majesty has bet six of his best horses that you will win. Do you agree to the match?"

46
00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:27.496
"I do," Hamlet answered.

47
00:04:27.497 --> 00:04:34.496
"Then please come to the great hall and choose your sword," said the nobleman. He bowed and left the room.

48
00:04:34.497 --> 00:04:40.742
Hamlet turned back to Horatio and said, "Something about this makes me uneasy."

49
00:04:40.743 --> 00:04:46.822
Horatio was worried too. "Tell the king you've changed your mind," he urged his friend.

50
00:04:46.823 --> 00:04:55.496
"No, it's all right," Hamlet replied. "I believe whatever happens is meant to happen. It's fate. Let's go."

51
00:04:55.497 --> 00:05:02.552
In the great hall, the entire court was assembled. Several swords were laid out on a long table.

52
00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:06.274
Laertes pretended to examine all the swords.

53
00:05:06.275 --> 00:05:11.719
Finally he chose the one he had dipped in poison. Hamlet chose another.

54
00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:21.886
"If Hamlet makes a hit," announced Claudius, "I will reward him by putting a pearl richer than any in the royal crown in his cup of wine!"

55
00:05:21.887 --> 00:05:28.256
The drums and trumpets sounded, and the duel began. Hamlet quickly scored two hits.

56
00:05:28.257 --> 00:05:36.867
The king picked up a cup of wine and dropped a pearl into it. But only Claudius and Laertes knew the pearl was poisoned!

57
00:05:36.868 --> 00:05:40.764
"Come, Hamlet—drink," said the king.

58
00:05:40.765 --> 00:05:46.811
Breathing hard, Hamlet replied, "I'll drink later. Let's continue, Laertes."

59
00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:51.843
"I will drink to your health, Hamlet," said Gertrude, picking up the poisoned cup.

60
00:05:51.844 --> 00:05:55.454
"No! Don't drink it, Gertrude!" cried the king.

61
00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:58.867
"Why not?" she replied and took a sip.

62
00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:03.256
Claudius watched in dismay. "It's too late!" he thought.

63
00:06:03.257 --> 00:06:10.359
At that moment Laertes struck Hamlet, drawing blood. They scuffled and both dropped their swords.

64
00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:16.042
Hamlet lunged for Laertes' sword and swung, leaving a red gash on his opponent's skin.

65
00:06:16.043 --> 00:06:22.136
"Why are they both bleeding?" exclaimed Horatio, jumping up. "The sword tips should be blunt!"

66
00:06:22.137 --> 00:06:25.843
Just then Gertrude slumped forward in her chair.

67
00:06:25.844 --> 00:06:29.296
"Mother, what's wrong?" cried Hamlet.

68
00:06:29.297 --> 00:06:37.049
"It's the wine . . . Oh dear Hamlet . . . ," moaned the queen. Then she collapsed to the floor.

69
00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.439
"What villain did this?" Hamlet howled.

70
00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:47.971
"He's in this room," said Laertes. "And you and I shall not live much longer. The sword you hold is poisoned.

71
00:06:47.972 --> 00:06:54.430
I am caught by my own trap, and your mother is poisoned too. The king's to blame!"

72
00:06:54.431 --> 00:06:58.161
"Then poison, do your work!" roared Hamlet.

73
00:06:58.162 --> 00:07:05.326
He stabbed Claudius, and grabbing the cup of poisoned wine, he forced the liquid down the king's throat.

74
00:07:05.327 --> 00:07:09.715
Claudius shuddered and took his last breath.

75
00:07:09.716 --> 00:07:20.279
"Hamlet," murmured Laertes, "let us forgive one another to save our souls. I forgive you." He closed his eyes and was still.

76
00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:30.185
"As I forgive you," said Hamlet weakly. "Horatio, please tell my story after I'm gone."

77
00:07:30.186 --> 00:07:33.372
Outside the castle drums sounded.

78
00:07:33.373 --> 00:07:36.841
"Fortinbras is coming!" shouted a servant.

79
00:07:36.842 --> 00:07:45.563
"Let Fortinbras be king of Denmark," whispered Hamlet. "I am dying . . . The rest is silence."

80
00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:54.397
"Good night, sweet prince," murmured Horatio with tears in his eyes, "and flights of angels sing thee to thy rest."

81
00:07:54.398 --> 00:07:59.993
Horatio looked up as Fortinbras and some of his soldiers marched into the great hall.

82
00:07:59.994 --> 00:08:02.873
They were horrified by what they saw.

83
00:08:02.874 --> 00:08:09.335
"I will explain to you the terrible deeds that led to this sad sight," Horatio said mournfully.

84
00:08:09.336 --> 00:08:13.629
"Fortinbras, you are to be the new king of Denmark."

85
00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:21.723
"I have some claim upon this kingdom, but it is with sorrow I accept my fortune," declared Fortinbras.

86
00:08:21.724 --> 00:08:25.672
"Hamlet would have made a noble king."

